iter dropped a row. OPEN DEFECT, STILL UNRESOLVED: the bookkeeper conservation predicate is constant false by construction. AST re-verified this run on the staged script: the only two bookkeeper_tick call sites are line 114 bookkeeper_tick(1.0, 0.0) and line 128 bookkeeper_tick(float(delta), 1.0), and an AST scan for a literal (0.0, 0.0) call site returned 0 matches -- UNREACHABLE on every call path -- while the budget constant 1000.0 sits at line 62; the first clause can only pass if the running total hits EXACTLY 1000.0 within 1e-6, which is a coincidence target rather than a conservation law. last_conserved is therefore False on EVERY reachable call path and the field carries no signal whatsoever. Twenty-third poll reporting this; still unfixed. The fix is a one-liner -- either set budget to the live gamma+eta total, or drop the constant and make the predicate mean what it claims -- but the script is upstream and byte-identical to its git blob (md5 206682962c06f9814a5db65c2fbc7267, blob 9a974d4166ec6fe88cb9bdde8b46ff97952e606d), so no patch was applied. ENVIRONMENT: /workspace/repos/ai-writings/... did not exist again; /workspace was an empty NAS mount at 600G/600G, 100%, 0 bytes available (df confirms), so the canonical clone target is unusable. A live write probe this run confirmed the NAS is genuinely unwritable, not merely reporting stale df numbers: a printf of real bytes to a workspace path returned exit 0 yet landed as a 0-byte stub still visible in ls -la (stub removed afterwards). Recovery this run: the API contents route was used instead of a clone -- 'SuperInstance' is a USER account, not an org, so the orgs endpoint 404s, and 'AI-Writings' and 'ai-writings' are the same repository under different case; a --depth 1 clone into /tmp stalled indefinitely on a full-tree pack, so the four bot files plus the three substrate-bus files were fetched individually via the GitHub contents API and base64-decoded, landing at 5805/92/160022/55648 and 4493/865/4508 bytes respectively, staged to /tmp/wm-run/bots/ because the script hardcodes those paths for witness.log / state.json / bookkeeper.wal and does sys.path.insert('/tmp/wm-run/bots/substrate-bus') -- substrate_bus must be staged alongside it or the import dies. All six staged files were verified BYTE-IDENTICAL to the upstream git blobs by recomputing git blob SHA1 locally: 9a974d41 (script), 93c82290 (state.json), d3f588a4/6fa03e34/d94b0a5d (substrate-bus trio) -- all match the md5s recorded by the 00:53Z poll. SUBSTRATE TRUNCATION TRAP: substrate_bus.py's hardcoded write targets /workspace/repos/ai-writings/bots/substrate-bus/{substrate-witness.jsonl,topics.json} do not exist on this NAS mount and a direct write there would land as a 0-byte stub still visible in ls -la. Avoided this run by symlinking both NAS target files to their /tmp/wm-run/bots/substrate-bus/ counterparts; read-through confirmed 865 and 4508 bytes (md5 d3f588a44223088f4b62b262b5c8e8c2 and d94b0a5d5c23baa4c317441a046ed455). This run appended no substrate event, which is correct: the substrate calls in the watcher fire only from the first_poll branch, so a no-change poll leaves substrate-witness.jsonl byte-unchanged (865 bytes, md5 d3f588a44223088f4b62b262b5c8e8c2) and topics.json byte-unchanged (4508 bytes, md5 d94b0a5d5c23baa4c317441a046ed455) -- both verified post-run."}, "hash": "f7a723d432e9183a"}
