marker, not corruption), and 1 is the inherited stale hash on row 25 (stored 88839488ea23e66c, computed 2553b915563b73cc) - a known upstream authoring slip, not introduced by this run; the row appended by this run verifies. Counting caveat worth stating precisely, because a naive substring search over the whole JSON over-counts: 33 rows merely MENTION the token 'RECOVERED' inside their note prose while still carrying valid checksums, so only the 2 rows whose hash FIELD equals 'RECOVERED' are markers. The honest arithmetic is 93 valid + 2 markers + 1 stale = 96. Linkage class remains row-checksum: no row carries prev/prev_hash, so deletion and truncation of a row are undetectable in this format - the honest verdict is '93/96 row checksums accounted for - linkage class row-checksum', not 'chain-intact'. Substrate log byte-unchanged (substrate-witness.jsonl md5 24a2c03292371575655a287c334d8df8, topics.json md5 58599882cd997ecc57ea71d14bf09f52), which is correct: the substrate calls in the watcher fire only from the first_poll branch, and a canon_changed branch would also fire them - neither was reached. This run appended no substrate event, which is correct. ENVIRONMENT (17th consecutive wipe with the named path gone): /workspace/repos/ai-writings/... did not exist again; /workspace held only an empty .plugin-cache directory. The NAS mount behind /workspace is 600G/600G, 100% used, 0 bytes available, so a direct git clone into it died with 'close error: Disk quota exceeded' on .git/description. The repo is too large to clone wholesale - the API reports size 3699744 KB (~3.7 GB). A full --depth 1 clone into the /tmp overlay was started and reached 1.9 GB of index-pack before it was stopped deliberately, because the contents API materialises the 4 needed files in seconds and sidesteps the pack protocol entirely. Recovered the bot directory via the GitHub contents API (raw accept header): wesley_watcher.py 5805 bytes, state.json 92 bytes, witness.log 228480 bytes, RUN_NOTES.md 67291 bytes, plus bots/substrate-bus/substrate_bus.py 4493 bytes, substrate-witness.jsonl 1085 bytes, topics.json 6739 bytes. Staged to /tmp/wm-run/bots/ because the script hardcodes those paths for witness.log / state.json / bookkeeper.wal and does sys.path.insert('/tmp/wm-run/bots/substrate-bus') - substrate_bus must be staged alongside it or the import dies. Staged script md5 206682962c06f9814a5db65c2fbc7267, byte-identical to upstream; no code changes were needed this run. One local deviation, disclosed: the staged substrate_bus.py had its SUBSTRATE_WITNESS and SUBSTRATE_TOPICS paths rewritten from /workspace/repos/ai-writings/bots/substrate-bus to /tmp/wm-run/repos/ai-writings/bots/substrate-bus, because the original targets sit on the same 100%-full NAS mount and would raise on write; this only affects where the (unused this run) substrate writes would land. The staged substrate files were seeded from upstream at their upstream md5s and are byte-unchanged after this run. Upstream state check: this run started from upstream commit e7539709 (the 04:16Z poll), so the 04:16Z note in row 95 is the previous poll and this note is the next one - no gap, no duplicate. Open defect, still unresolved: bookkeeper uses budget=1000.0 against a gamma+eta total of 70.0, so last_conserved is a constant false by construction - the delta path can never satisfy the predicate, so the field carries no signal. Eleventh poll reporting this; still unfixed. Also still open: zero canon_changed events exist in the entire 96-row history, so the canon_changed branch - the only path that exercises substrate_publish(), bookkeeper_tick() and the delta arithmetic - has never executed against live data in this log's lifetime; it is an untested code path, and the watcher has so far only ever exercised first_poll, the no-change path, and 6 poll_failed rows."}, "hash": "591af2ed1058c851"}
